WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.042
- I mean, being from St. Louis,

00:00:01.042 --> 00:00:04.587
like Bob, Bob Gibson and

00:00:04.629 --> 00:00:07.048
Lou Brock were like the first Black

00:00:07.048 --> 00:00:08.550
ballplayers that I was really introduced to.

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:11.511
- I'm from Atlanta and, you know, Hank Aaron,

00:00:11.511 --> 00:00:13.179
you know, he was the Braves.

00:00:18.601 --> 00:00:22.272
- A number of legendary,

00:00:22.272 --> 00:00:25.316
you know, Black ballplayers passed away recently.

00:00:25.316 --> 00:00:27.777
Hank Aaron being the latest.

00:00:27.777 --> 00:00:29.904
Hall of Famer. Bob Gibson, Hall of

00:00:29.904 --> 00:00:32.449
Famer. Lou Brock, Hall of Famer.

00:00:32.449 --> 00:00:34.242
That's our legacy. In this sport,

00:00:34.284 --> 00:00:35.952
you have to be great.

00:00:35.952 --> 00:00:38.038
They were great.

00:00:38.038 --> 00:00:40.498
And they did it the right way.

00:00:40.498 --> 00:00:41.708
We look at activism now.

00:00:41.708 --> 00:00:44.377
We know Hank Aaron's

00:00:44.377 --> 00:00:46.921
legacy as a player.

00:00:46.921 --> 00:00:49.299
But he didn't stop there.

00:00:49.299 --> 00:00:50.592
He was coming off the likes of

00:00:50.633 --> 00:00:52.719
Jackie Robinson and

00:00:52.761 --> 00:00:54.387
those that have come before him. And

00:00:54.387 --> 00:00:56.556
activism. I am more than a player.

00:00:56.556 --> 00:00:58.099
I am more than a uniform.

00:00:58.099 --> 00:00:59.893
- [Dwight Gooden:] I grew up in Tampa.

00:00:59.893 --> 00:01:01.144
And in the late '60s, early

00:01:01.144 --> 00:01:03.688
'70s, the Reds had spring training in Tampa.

00:01:03.688 --> 00:01:05.899
I saw the Reds play, and I actually got to meet

00:01:05.899 --> 00:01:07.650
Joe Morgan and know him personally.

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:10.779
Bob Gibson. Had many conversations with Bob.

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:12.739
Hank was the first guy

00:01:12.822 --> 00:01:15.158
that I did a card show with in 1984.

00:01:15.158 --> 00:01:16.743
I came up in '84 but became

00:01:16.743 --> 00:01:18.870
a fan of baseball in '69.

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:20.789
You know, at that time, and just

00:01:20.830 --> 00:01:24.000
watching the games, told my dad, seeing these guys play,
"Hey, Dad," like you said,

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:25.835
they did it the right way. On the field

00:01:25.835 --> 00:01:28.171
and off the field. Those are my guys.

00:01:28.171 --> 00:01:34.344
When I was around those guys, man, it brought out the little 
kid in me because I remember watching those guys [inaudible].

00:01:34.344 --> 00:01:36.304
But like you just said, those are the true

00:01:36.304 --> 00:01:40.350
legends. And not only set examples on the field but off the field as well.

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:42.936
And they will be missed because that's the foundation.

00:01:42.936 --> 00:01:48.316
And I'm glad hopefully to continue to do something throughout the season, during the summer so these guys can

00:01:48.316 --> 00:01:49.400
get the recognition that they deserve.

00:01:49.818 --> 00:01:51.778
- Growing up in the Bay Area, you had two options.

00:01:51.778 --> 00:01:53.321
You had Little League or Oakland Babe Ruth.

00:01:54.364 --> 00:01:55.949
And I played Oakland Babe Ruth.

00:01:55.949 --> 00:01:57.492
But I would always say to myself,

00:01:57.534 --> 00:01:58.785
you know, "Why is Babe Ruth

00:01:58.785 --> 00:02:00.453
so revered

00:02:00.453 --> 00:02:02.831
when Hank Aaron is the Home Run King?"

00:02:02.831 --> 00:02:04.916
You know, it would never rest well with

00:02:04.916 --> 00:02:07.168
me other than, you know, obviously the legend of

00:02:07.168 --> 00:02:10.296
Babe Ruth, you know, drinking beers,

00:02:10.588 --> 00:02:13.299
eating hot dogs, coming up on one leg

00:02:13.299 --> 00:02:14.467
and hitting the ball out the ballpark.

00:02:14.467 --> 00:02:16.219
I mean, that's legendary stuff. But

00:02:16.219 --> 00:02:18.555
I'm like, but Hank didn't do that.

00:02:18.972 --> 00:02:20.890
You know, he stayed

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:23.059
the course, was an activist,

00:02:23.476 --> 00:02:25.812
you know, played in Milwaukee

00:02:26.146 --> 00:02:28.273
and then over to Atlanta.

00:02:28.273 --> 00:02:29.357
And he is the real Home Run King.

00:02:29.357 --> 00:02:31.484
So why are we, why are we, you know,

00:02:31.484 --> 00:02:33.403
revering, you know, Babe Ruth so much?

00:02:34.571 --> 00:02:36.322
So my favorite player was Rickey Henderson,

00:02:36.656 --> 00:02:37.949
who was chasing Lou Brock.

00:02:37.949 --> 00:02:39.868
So that legacy was just

00:02:39.868 --> 00:02:41.494
always being passed down.

00:02:41.494 --> 00:02:42.579
You know, who was in front of me?

00:02:42.954 --> 00:02:44.164
Who do I have to look up to?

00:02:44.164 --> 00:02:45.248
And what do I have to chase?

00:02:45.582 --> 00:02:46.374
What is the legacy

00:02:46.374 --> 00:02:47.625
do I leave next?

00:02:48.334 --> 00:02:50.170
You had those guys that we knew

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:51.963
about. Bob Gibson, legendary.

00:02:52.213 --> 00:02:53.006
I mean, you let Willie

00:02:53.006 --> 00:02:54.632
Mays tell it, Bob Gibson,

00:02:54.674 --> 00:02:55.842
you know, didn't wear glasses on the

00:02:55.842 --> 00:02:57.760
mound on purpose, but off the field he

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:00.889
had on glasses.
[Laughing]
So that's why he was flipping people up and down.

00:03:00.889 --> 00:03:05.935
You know, Willie getting in a high-pitched voice, "Hey, you wear glasses?"
[Laughing]

00:03:05.935 --> 00:03:07.896
You know, all the great legendary stories.

00:03:07.896 --> 00:03:10.398
So, for me, it was,

00:03:10.732 --> 00:03:13.985
you know, knowing that they did

00:03:13.985 --> 00:03:15.528
it before me, knowing their story

00:03:15.528 --> 00:03:16.821
and all the things that they went

00:03:16.821 --> 00:03:18.615
through, how can I make my mark

00:03:19.115 --> 00:03:21.367
so I don't disrespect them and their

00:03:21.367 --> 00:03:23.828
legacy? Because it wasn't about me,

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:25.580
you know, in a uniform.

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:25.830
Yes,

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:26.623
I'm Black in a uniform

00:03:26.623 --> 00:03:28.416
and I have to make my mark, but I'm making

00:03:28.416 --> 00:03:30.210
my mark because I'm leaving a legacy.

00:03:30.877 --> 00:03:31.377
I'm leaving,

00:03:31.377 --> 00:03:32.879
I'm living in their legacy.

00:03:32.879 --> 00:03:35.548
I'm living in their shadows.
- You know,

00:03:35.548 --> 00:03:38.927
growing up and hearing the stories from my

00:03:38.927 --> 00:03:42.889
uncle, who, my grandfather, so, they said

00:03:42.889 --> 00:03:44.724
my grandfather actually played against

00:03:44.724 --> 00:03:48.228
Hank Aaron in like some of the, before

00:03:48.228 --> 00:03:49.938
Hank went to the Negro Leagues, I

00:03:49.938 --> 00:03:55.360
guess he played in like these
- [Offscreen:] The barnstorming days.

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:59.072
- [Ryan Howard:] kinda old-model leagues in Alabama.
And my grandfather played against him.

00:03:59.113 --> 00:04:01.532
So my uncle would tell me these stories

00:04:01.532 --> 00:04:03.034
all the time about, you know, watching

00:04:03.034 --> 00:04:04.369
my grandfather and playing against

00:04:04.369 --> 00:04:05.787
Hank Aaron, and all this kind of stuff.

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:08.248
So watching what he did

00:04:09.791 --> 00:04:11.751
and actually being able to

00:04:13.586 --> 00:04:16.631
get a Hank, an award named after Hank

00:04:16.673 --> 00:04:20.093
Aaron is kind of, it's surreal. Because it's kind of

00:04:20.093 --> 00:04:24.514
like, I feel like I can't even take this

00:04:24.514 --> 00:04:26.599
because of what this man accomplished.

00:04:26.599 --> 00:04:30.353
Like, I'm not even on the same level

00:04:30.353 --> 00:04:33.147
or playing field as a Hank Aaron.

00:04:33.147 --> 00:04:37.652
To have 755 home runs, over 2,200 RBI.

00:04:37.652 --> 00:04:39.779
Like, that was the bar.

00:04:40.571 --> 00:04:42.031
And as a power hitter,

00:04:42.031 --> 00:04:43.408
you're like, as soon as you

00:04:43.408 --> 00:04:46.411
get to league, I'm chasing Hank Aaron.

00:04:46.411 --> 00:04:48.454
I'm trying to eclipse him. Because that

00:04:48.454 --> 00:04:50.873
was the bar that was set.

00:04:50.873 --> 00:04:52.917
Like, stopping and thinking about my

00:04:52.917 --> 00:04:55.586
career now, like 382 home runs. Man,

00:04:55.586 --> 00:04:56.504
that's a lot of home runs.

00:04:57.130 --> 00:04:59.549
755 home runs, bro?

00:04:59.924 --> 00:05:02.302
That is insanity.

00:05:03.219 --> 00:05:04.095
It's insanity.

00:05:04.095 --> 00:05:05.513
To be that consistent,

00:05:05.513 --> 00:05:07.307
to be able to do what he did

00:05:07.682 --> 00:05:09.309
over the course of his career.

00:05:09.767 --> 00:05:12.186
It's what we all strive for.

00:05:12.186 --> 00:05:13.604
Like for me, it was kind of like,

00:05:13.604 --> 00:05:14.981
I just want to do my part to say

00:05:14.981 --> 00:05:17.191
thank you for paving the way.

00:05:17.775 --> 00:05:19.736
And that's going to be me going out

00:05:19.736 --> 00:05:22.113
here every single day, playing the game

00:05:22.113 --> 00:05:23.614
the right way, preparing myself the

00:05:23.614 --> 00:05:26.409
right way and trying to go out here and

00:05:26.576 --> 00:05:28.703
do things to the best of my ability.

00:05:29.162 --> 00:05:31.039
Because that's what you did.

00:05:31.039 --> 00:05:32.457
That's what these guys did.

00:05:33.082 --> 00:05:35.585
And Bob Gibson and Hank Aaron, those

00:05:35.585 --> 00:05:38.129
guys, Lou Brock, it's the precedent

00:05:38.129 --> 00:05:42.175
that's set. It's that pride of being

00:05:42.175 --> 00:05:44.969
a Black baseball player to where now

00:05:44.969 --> 00:05:46.721
it's the phenomenon of,

00:05:47.221 --> 00:05:48.765
you know, there's very few

00:05:48.765 --> 00:05:52.352
and far between, but to see Kyle,

00:05:52.352 --> 00:05:54.645
to see Devin, to see some other young,

00:05:54.645 --> 00:05:57.065
Black ballplayers in the game today,

00:05:58.066 --> 00:06:00.610
the future is very, very bright.

00:06:00.610 --> 00:06:01.611
- Devin,

00:06:01.611 --> 00:06:04.113
We'll start with you. What is

00:06:04.113 --> 00:06:07.116
your history of the game? Generally as we get

00:06:07.116 --> 00:06:08.534
further and further away from those

00:06:08.534 --> 00:06:10.661
generations, the less they're talked about.

00:06:11.412 --> 00:06:14.082
So how did you learn about, you

00:06:14.082 --> 00:06:15.166
know, the greats in the game

00:06:15.708 --> 00:06:18.795
in light of the ones that we've

00:06:18.795 --> 00:06:20.088
lost, that have passed recently?

00:06:21.422 --> 00:06:22.507
- [Devin Williams:] I mean, being from St.

00:06:22.507 --> 00:06:25.802
Louis, like Bob, Bob Gibson

00:06:25.802 --> 00:06:28.262
and Lou Brock was like the first

00:06:28.262 --> 00:06:29.555
Black ballplayers that I was really

00:06:29.555 --> 00:06:31.391
introduced to, you know. I grew up

00:06:31.391 --> 00:06:34.310
watching the old black and white films of

00:06:34.310 --> 00:06:36.938
Bob Gibson, you know, striking out

00:06:36.938 --> 00:06:38.564
18 or whatever it was in a

00:06:38.564 --> 00:06:40.400
game, you know. The things like that.

00:06:41.109 --> 00:06:44.278
Lou Brock just, you know,

00:06:44.278 --> 00:06:45.530
being the game-changer that he

00:06:45.530 --> 00:06:47.240
was in all facets of the game.

00:06:48.866 --> 00:06:50.785
I mean, those guys really kind of were

00:06:50.785 --> 00:06:53.329
the first ones to, you know, that I could

00:06:53.329 --> 00:06:55.456
see in myself, you know, that I'm watching

00:06:55.456 --> 00:06:58.960
on TV and I can see the similarities

00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:00.628
between us, you know. Whether you like

00:07:00.628 --> 00:07:02.046
it or not, you just, you pay attention

00:07:02.046 --> 00:07:03.339
more to people that look like you.

00:07:04.048 --> 00:07:06.467
Then that kind of led into, growing up,

00:07:06.467 --> 00:07:09.429
I read Doc's autobiography growing up.

00:07:09.429 --> 00:07:11.139
You know, he's a pitcher, I'm a pitcher.

00:07:11.139 --> 00:07:13.724
I identify with him, you know, and that

00:07:13.724 --> 00:07:16.769
just kind of kept it rolling.

00:07:17.019 --> 00:07:19.105
Getting into when

00:07:19.105 --> 00:07:20.606
I actually started playing,

00:07:20.606 --> 00:07:22.275
then I was following you two.

00:07:22.275 --> 00:07:23.693
You know, Ryan being from St.

00:07:23.693 --> 00:07:25.736
Louis, you played shortstop, which

00:07:25.736 --> 00:07:27.029
was what I played at the time.

00:07:27.029 --> 00:07:29.031
You know, I had to have all your cleats.

00:07:29.031 --> 00:07:30.783
You know, I tried to have the batting gloves

00:07:30.783 --> 00:07:33.744
you had, man. Like, all that stuff. But,

00:07:34.745 --> 00:07:37.123
yeah, I mean, there's definitely, it's,

00:07:38.458 --> 00:07:39.750
to me, it's an honor to be like

00:07:39.750 --> 00:07:41.127
one of those guys, that some kid

00:07:41.127 --> 00:07:42.170
right now is going to be doing the

00:07:42.170 --> 00:07:43.463
same thing I'm doing right now

00:07:43.463 --> 00:07:45.631
hopefully in 15, 20 years.

00:07:51.762 --> 00:07:54.140
- [Kyle Lewis:] I'm from Atlanta and, you know, Hank

00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:56.142
Aaron, you know, he was the Braves.

00:07:56.142 --> 00:07:57.810
You know, when I grew up, you know,

00:07:57.810 --> 00:07:59.479
Hank Aaron was the gold

00:07:59.479 --> 00:08:02.356
standard for everything hitting.

00:08:02.356 --> 00:08:03.274
being from the South,

00:08:03.274 --> 00:08:03.983
You know,

00:08:03.983 --> 00:08:05.943
looking up to him, and he

00:08:05.943 --> 00:08:07.236
would always, you know, make efforts

00:08:07.236 --> 00:08:09.489
to be around, be in the community.

00:08:09.489 --> 00:08:10.907
And I got the opportunity to meet him

00:08:10.907 --> 00:08:13.826
one time, and I just remember being,

00:08:13.826 --> 00:08:15.786
chills going through my body, you know?

00:08:15.786 --> 00:08:18.498
And so it's, you know, different

00:08:18.498 --> 00:08:20.416
experiences you have, you know,

00:08:20.416 --> 00:08:21.792
throughout your lifetime, and watching

00:08:21.792 --> 00:08:23.878
you guys, you know, with the Phillies.

00:08:23.878 --> 00:08:25.505
You guys' legendary runs and

00:08:26.255 --> 00:08:28.090
screaming at the TV and whatnot.

00:08:28.090 --> 00:08:30.635
Man, these things that they

00:08:30.635 --> 00:08:31.928
set, they set the table.

00:08:31.928 --> 00:08:33.346
And so I would run around my house,

00:08:33.846 --> 00:08:35.348
you know, doing, you know, Ryan Howard

00:08:35.348 --> 00:08:38.601
home run trots, you know, and, trying to

00:08:38.601 --> 00:08:40.937
see, you know, what I, how I was going to celebrate when I

00:08:40.937 --> 00:08:42.313
crossed home plate and things like that.

00:08:42.313 --> 00:08:44.815
So, you know, hopefully we can just

00:08:44.815 --> 00:08:46.692
continue to be the next in line in

00:08:46.692 --> 00:08:48.110
whatever way that may be, you know.

00:08:48.110 --> 00:08:50.321
And that may not be being the greatest

00:08:50.321 --> 00:08:52.365
player ever, but, you know, just in

00:08:52.365 --> 00:08:53.741
the way that we represent ourselves.

00:08:54.325 --> 00:08:55.576
Talk to people, play

00:08:55.576 --> 00:08:57.119
the game the right way,

00:08:57.119 --> 00:08:58.538
you know, and wear the uniform the right way.

00:08:58.538 --> 00:09:01.624
So I definitely just try to

00:09:01.624 --> 00:09:02.833
pick up on those little things

00:09:02.833 --> 00:09:04.418
and continue to

00:09:05.211 --> 00:09:06.754
give my respect whenever I can.

00:09:06.754 --> 00:09:08.089
But Hank Aaron

00:09:08.089 --> 00:09:09.799
was a tough one to see

00:09:09.799 --> 00:09:12.301
go cuz, man, he was the gold standard

00:09:12.301 --> 00:09:13.344
really.

00:09:13.344 --> 00:09:14.762
- Young gems on here.

00:09:14.762 --> 00:09:16.639
You know what? This game is in

00:09:16.639 --> 00:09:18.182
some very good hands.

00:09:18.182 --> 00:09:20.184
I mean, if you two are setting

00:09:20.184 --> 00:09:21.936
a standard of what it's like to

00:09:21.936 --> 00:09:25.231
be a Black baseball player, this

00:09:25.231 --> 00:09:27.108
game is in some really good hands.

00:09:27.108 --> 00:09:29.652
If you guys are the future, I really

00:09:29.652 --> 00:09:31.237
love the way baseball is going

00:09:31.237 --> 00:09:33.239
for us Black athletes in baseball, for real.

